prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–8 sentences: I just promoted a backlog item to a feature spec via `git mv` and the spec is fully drafted (Requirements / Test Strategy / Design / Implementation Plan all populated). What do you do next?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers six behaviors. (1) **Recognises the populated-spec rule.** A spec on disk is *not* a reviewed spec. The agent enters the planning phase, not the dev loop, even though the spec is fully drafted. (2) **Confirms readiness with the user or iterates** on the spec — drafted does not mean approved; the planning round may revise Requirements / Test Strategy / Design / Implementation Plan before any code work. (3) **Commits the spec as `plan(<feature>): initial spec`** — the `plan(<feature>):` commit type (or equivalent that names the feature scope) is required; literal subject phrasing is flexible. The commit touches only the spec, no code. (4) **Pushes the feature branch** to its upstream. (5) **Runs the Planning Review Gate** — opens or updates the PR/CR with body shape **Why** + **Spec summary** (Requirements / Test Strategy / Design / Implementation Plan one-liners — note the order: tests sit between requirements and design) + **Open questions**. Opens as a normal review, not draft. The internal-marker grep reruns against title + body before submission. (6) **Waits for the explicit planning → dev cue** ("spec looks good" / "start build" / "plan approved" / equivalent) before starting any implementation work. Wrong answers: starting implementation immediately because the spec is "done"; committing as `feat(...)` instead of `plan(<feature>):`; opening as draft; skipping the Planning Review Gate; including only Why + Approach (the agent-dev body shape) instead of the planning shape; chaining straight into the dev loop without waiting for the cue.
tools: claude,kiro
